ure of these systems  there's a tight symphony of events that  need to occur  to get our customers their groceries on  time and correctly  and any disruption can prevent us from  doing that  additionally the nature of the business  that we serve has changed as the  pandemic  goes on these tools allow our customers  to get groceries safely  and a disruption to that is uh something  we don't want  so uptime is of a mission critical  concern and we want a better tooling to  address it  we also have frequently changing needs  due to the nature of this  business each change just requested by  the business  simple as it may be may uh require  complex changes across the entire  all of these applications and the entire  ecosystem  so we knew we wanted to uh better serve  uh the need for change  so we decided that we needed a new  digital fulfillment  services ecosystem we asked ourselves  as an engineering group what we wanted  to get out of a modernization journey  and rebuilding these systems we knew we  wanted to deliver work faster  recover from incidents sooner we wanted  to be  more scalable and resilient to the  realities and complexities of operating  a complex system we wanted to empower  our engineers  to grow and learn more parts of the  stack than they had ever participated in  before  and learn a lot of the new cloud native  tools that are available to them  we also wanted single sources of truth  for every domain within digital  fulfillment  so we decided that a microservices  approach met these needs  that said we knew that the road to  microservices was fraught with risk that  we could create a distributed monolith  or a situation worse than what we  already had  so we came up with a simple set of  principles to help us  avoid these traps and be successful in  our journey  we wanted our services to be stateless  we wanted to reduce code complexity with  everything that we did  we wanted to encapsulate functionality  within services to reduce operational  com