 as well as uh understand uh our data in  a way we never had before  one of the other competencies that we  built was feature flagging  specifically utilizing a tool that we  built ourselves  for uh toggling features on and off in a  way that makes sense for heb  and we open sourced this tool with the  help of joe's team  we worked with him to introduce the role  of  sre to our teams we embedded these  partners on our squads  and they focused their efforts on  helping us define sli's slos  and refining our alerts until they were  actionable and meaningful  they also helped us to find good error  budget policies  and our stakeholders agreed to them  which allow us to change our behavior  should we exceed our error budget we  also refined our incident response  process  so that we can put our engineers on call  for what they build  and on healthy rotations so they can  really demonstrate that full  end to end ownership  the next thing that we did was build a  practice around chaos engineering we did  this by bringing in gremlin  who through the use of guided game days  helped us build a mindset around  introducing failure scientifically  to what we build this allows us to  validate all of our assumptions in our  designs  and really understand how our system is  going to perform when failures do occur  this has grown into red versus blue  exercises where we can practice incident  response  and is now growing even further into us  automating our chaos engineering  so we're at the point where our new  services are in production they're  rolling out  to stores and by the end of august our  full digital fulfillment ecosystem  should be live in every curbside  location  at heb  some of the key learnings at this stage  of the journey have been that change  safety is vital  as you start to move faster it increases  the risk that you can push an error to  production and so you need  a lot of automation around your testing  so you can move at that speed  as well as things like canary analysis  in your deployment pipeline to prevent  you from pushing things to production  that cause issues  you need to size your services for your  teams and what i mean by that is  as you remove complexity from your code  bases  you're introducing it into the operation  of your services  and not every team needs thousands of  microservices  so make sure that how you size your  services is something you can  successfully operate  we also learned that distributed systems  are really hard a lot of our issues here  at the end have been around  fine tuning our domain driven design and  redrawing those boundary lines and you  can't be afraid to fail  but this is where that validation of  your assumptions becomes key  as you need to refine as you go and not  have to redesign your system at the end  and build the muscle around distributed  systems ultimately  this doesn't end this is your new normal  we're doing this so we can better serve  our business but understanding that  there isn't a finish line to all of this  is  a key learning for us one of the most  important things we learned  is that focusing on the people is key to  a successful digital transformation and  that's because  the people that do the work are the ones  that bear the burden  of learning all of these new  technologies changing how they deliver  work the technical part of this is the  easiest part  providing a space for your people to  fail safely  to learn and investing in them is how  you  navigate this change successfully  so we're at the the next stage at the  end of august joe and i will be working  on our strategy to take what we've built  on gke  and transition it to gke on prim and  then  the move that to the edge in our stores  so that we have that ultimate level of  reliability our services can run right  next to our customers  and weather the storm when there's  unexpected events like a fiber cut  thank you for attending our talk if you  have any questions feel free to reach  out to joe or myself we appreciate you


